<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teatro San Cassiano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teatro San Cassiano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the first public opera house in the world, opened in 1637 by the Tron family, where audiences paid for admission instead of attending by invitation at court. Its success led to many more commercial opera houses in Venice, which became the leading centre of opera in the 17th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was demolished in 1812.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-san-cassiano.jpg" alt="Teatro San Cassiano" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secchi Smith, ©Teatro San Cassiano Ltd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_San_Cassiano_reimagined.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the Tron family for spoken comedies stood on the site by 1581, in the parish of San Cassiano in the Santa Croce district, not far from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a>. Letters of that year already mention its boxes, later a key feature of the Italian opera house, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> began to demand that such theatres be strong and safe so that no collapse could happen. The Council closed it in 1585, together with the Michiel family's theatre; it probably reopened after 1607, and fires destroyed it in 1629 and 1633.</p><p>In 1636 the brothers Ettore and Francesco Tron told the authorities that they meant to open a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> for music. It opened in 1637 with L'Andromeda, with music by Francesco Manelli and a libretto by Benedetto Ferrari, and it was the first opera house where each spectator bought a ticket. No image of the building survives, but a notary's document of 1658 and later surveys point to 153 boxes in five tiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1639 Francesco Cavalli became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>'s leading composer. His Le nozze di Teti e di Peleo of that year is the first fully surviving opera written for the house, and works such as La Didone, L'Egisto, L'Ormindo and Giasone followed. Pietro Andrea Ziani, Marc'Antonio Ziani, Antonio Gianettini and Tomaso Albinoni also worked there, and in the 18th century Albinoni's long collaboration kept opera going, with works by Antonio Lotti and Baldassare Galuppi among others.</p><p>The old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was small: its proscenium was a little over 8 metres wide and its stage about 6.5 metres deep. A new theatre designed by Francesco Bognolo opened in 1763 with La morte di Dimone by Antonio Tozzi, its stage made about 3 metres deeper by demolishing two small houses behind it. In 1776 Giacomo Casanova described its fifth-tier boxes as a place of vice. The last known season was that of 1798, and in 1805 the French closed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After its demolition in 1812 its site became the Albrizzi garden. A project to reconstruct the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of 1637 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, conceived and financed by the English entrepreneur and musicologist Paul Atkin, was launched in June 2019 with a conference, an exhibition and a concert, with period stage machinery and moving sets planned and the formal support of the Comune of Venice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teatro San Cassiano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Venice opened the first public opera house, the <b>Teatro San Cassiano</b>, in 1637, when opera elsewhere was still largely a court entertainment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
